diagnosis_text:
A complete, layered investigation of the ospf_enterprise_static lab found no fault anywhere in the system at the time of this diagnosis.

**Affected component:** None — the DNS service (BIND/named on dns_server), the L3 routing fabric (OSPF/FRR on six routers), and the end-to-end host configuration on host_1_1_1_1 are all operating correctly.

**Mechanism:** Every diagnostic layer was examined. Phase A (infra_sweep + l2_snapshot) found no ACL drop rules, no static ARP poisoning, no duplicate MACs, no missing or down interfaces, and no resolver misconfiguration on any of the 15 devices. Phase B (ospf_snapshot) confirmed all six FRR routers have Full OSPF adjacencies and 10 valid routes each. Phase D (service_snapshot + pressure_sweep) confirmed that host_1_1_1_1 currently resolves web0.local through web3.local to the correct IPs (10.200.0.3–.6) and receives HTTP 200 responses for all four hostnames, with sub-10 ms response times.

**Localization:** On dns_server, `named` is running (PID 29) and the journald log is empty — the daemon has never crashed or logged an error. On host_1_1_1_1, resolv.conf correctly names 10.200.0.2, eth0 is UP with zero error/drop counters, and the direct-IP path to the DNS server is clear.

**Root cause:** No anomaly is present. The user's report of DNS name-resolution failure cannot be confirmed by any current evidence or historical trace; the claim is unconfirmed and likely reflected a transient condition (e.g., a brief startup delay or a one-time client-side glitch) that has since self-resolved.

**Fix:** No corrective action is required. The network is fully healthy.
